<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lord's Resistance Army</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1987">1987</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as LRA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lord's Resistance Army is a rebel group founded in northern Uganda in 1987 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-kony">Joseph Kony</a>, who claimed spiritual guidance and declared the aim of ruling by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-commandments">Ten Commandments</a>. Drawing on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acholi">Acholi</a> insurgency against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoweri-museveni">Yoweri Museveni</a>'s government, it waged a war of massacres, mutilation and the abduction of tens of thousands of children, displacing over a million people. After peace talks failed in 2008 it operated in the Congo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lords-resistance-army.jpg" alt="Lord's Resistance Army" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UN Security Council · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LRAweapons.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The conflict has roots in the divisions of Uganda. The south and east are largely inhabited by Bantu speakers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baganda">Baganda</a>, who worked closely with the British after the creation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> of Uganda in 1894, while the Nilotic-speaking Acholi of the north supplied much of the manual labour and came to make up a majority of the army. In 1986 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoweri-museveni">Yoweri Museveni</a>'s National Resistance Army won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugandan-bush-war">Ugandan Bush War</a> and took control of the country. Its operations in the north, such as Operation Simsim, involved burning, looting and killing.</p><p> In late 1987 a civilian movement led by Alice Auma arose, who presented herself as a prophet inspired by the Holy Spirit. Joseph Kony founded the Lord's Resistance Army in 1987 and preached a similar belief in protection from bullets, with a cross drawn in oil on the chest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In March 2002 the Ugandan army launched Operation Iron Fist against LRA bases in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, and the LRA retaliated by attacking refugee camps in northern Uganda and Eastern Equatoria, killing hundreds of civilians. In February 2004 an LRA unit attacked the Barlonyo camp for displaced people, killing over 300. In 2006 UNICEF estimated that the LRA had abducted at least 25,000 children. The International Criminal Court issued its first arrest warrants in 2005, against Kony, Vincent Otti, Okot Odhiambo, Raska Lukwiya and Dominic Ongwen, for crimes against humanity and war crimes.</p><p>Talks were held at Juba from 2006 to 2008, and a truce was signed on 26 August 2006 under which the LRA was to gather in Garamba National Park in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. Between December 2008 and March 2009 the forces of Uganda, the Congo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a> attacked the Garamba camps, and Kony was not captured. Revenge attacks by scattered LRA remnants killed over 1,000 people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The group relied heavily on abducting children to fill its ranks, using them as combatants and porters, and was organised in independent squads of 10 or 20 soldiers. Its ideology is disputed among scholars: many describe it as largely a cult of personality around Kony, mixing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and traditional spiritualism, and a report of 1997 found that it had no political programme the local population could understand. The conflict disrupted education and family life in Acholiland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lord's Resistance Army” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
